+## 4. Stage 2 still cannot compile `might fail <call>`
+
+`bootstrap/compiler.rb`'s `parse_primary` refuses the `might fail` guard: it
+falls through every arm and reaches `refuse(line, "unexpected token
+mightfail")`. Stage 1 compiles such a program; stage 2 does not. This is
+pre-existing and unrelated to `at`/`length of` — it was true before this phase and
+is unchanged by it.
+
+It is written down because the round-1 fix touched the table that decides it.
+`is_expression_start` in `bootstrap/compiler.rb` gained a `mightfail` entry so
+that it equals `Parser::kind_starts_expression` entry for entry; **the entry
+changes nothing today**, because the primary it names does not exist yet. It is
+there so the two tables are equal now rather than equal only until the day the
+primary is written — the alternative is a table whose next entry is added late,
+by which time nobody remembers that a *second* copy exists.
+
+**Why it matters.** A construct stage 1 accepts and stage 2 refuses is invisible
+to every byte-identity test, because those compare programs both engines accept.
+There is no `might fail` program in `corpus/`, and no S2/S3 test can catch this
+class of gap without one.
+
+**Suggested phase.** Teach `parse_primary` the guard, and add one corpus program
+that uses it, so the gap becomes a byte-identity case instead of an absence.
